In our proposed approach of combining pseudo-labelling with Bayesian Active Learning and human-in-the-loop, we evaluated the performance of three widely used heuristics: BALD, random, and entropy. A summary of the experimental results is tabulated in Table  2 , which highlights the performance of the BALD, Random, and Entropy heuristics across various epochs. The accuracy results from Fig.  12  indicate that entropy outperformed the other two heuristics concerning accuracy. At the 24th Bayesian Active Learning epoch, entropy achieved 96.00% accuracy, followed by random at 91.00% and BALD at 89.00%. Furthermore, entropy maintained the accuracy steadily, reaching a maximum accuracy of 97.95%. In comparison, random only reached a maximum accuracy of 97.11%, while BALD reached a maximum accuracy of 96.99%. We observed distinct patterns and outcomes for different heuristics. For the entropy heuristic, pseudo-labelling remained highly reliable across repeated runs, yielding on average approximately one incorrect pseudo-label correction across three random seeds, consistent with the behaviour illustrated in Fig.  13 . The random heuristic initially had a high number of incorrect pseudo-labels, then it reduced as the model started to generalise. However, the BALD heuristic demonstrated significant improvement, initially producing a few incorrect predicted pseudo-labels in the early epochs, which then stabilised. Nevertheless, towards the later stages, BALD did exhibit a slight increase in the count of incorrect pseudo-labels, reaching around 1 or 2 from epoch 14 onwards. BALD and random had 23 and 47 incorrect pseudo-labels, respectively, during experiments. The analysis of the train confusion matrices for the BALD and entropy heuristics shows that class imbalance persists in the dataset. In contrast, the random heuristic exhibits relatively balanced samples across the classes throughout the training process. Examining the test confusion matrices from Fig.  14  specifically for the BALD heuristic, we observe that patches are incorrectly predicted as pitted surface defects. Similarly, in the test confusion matrices for the entropy and random heuristics, we notice that pitted surface defects are incorrectly labelled as crazing. We emphasise that the human-in-the-loop mechanism was critical in correcting the errors in the pseudo-labelling process. Overall, our experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of combining active learning and pseudo-labelling with the added benefit of human intervention in correcting errors. The results also suggest that entropy is a reliable heuristic for active learning, with better performance than BALD and random. To assess robustness, the proposed Bayesian Active Learning + pseudo-labelling (Entropy heuristic) experiment was repeated across three random seeds (43, 123, and 456). The mean test accuracy was  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
